# Data files are stored BYTE-FOR-BYTE. No line-ending conversion, ever.
#
# Every result CSV is fingerprinted by sha256 (runner.manifest_sha256 reads in
# binary on purpose), and later stages refuse to resume when an input's hash
# changes. With core.autocrlf=true and no attributes, git rewrote these files on
# the way in (CRLF -> LF) and on the way out (LF -> CRLF, including the newlines
# INSIDE quoted model replies). The result was three different byte sequences for
# one frozen file:
#
#   working copy that Stages 4/5 fingerprinted   043d7954...
#   blob git actually stored                      43e72386...
#   what a Windows checkout/archive hands out     71cacb41...
#
# so a fresh clone could not resume against its own recorded fingerprints, and
# the stored replies were not the replies the model produced. -text stops git
# touching them.
*.csv     -text
*.tsv     -text
*.jsonl   -text
**/results/*.json  -text
**/data/*.json     -text

# Shell and SLURM scripts run on Linux (RCAC Scholar). With autocrlf a Windows
# checkout or `git archive` would hand bash CRLF lines, and `\r` breaks it.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.sbatch  text eol=lf
